Simu5G Framework Documentation
==============================

Building the documentation website
----------------------------------

The Simu5G documentation is built with the Sphinx tool (sphinx-doc.org).
A pipenv environment is set up automatically containing all the necessary python packages.

To build the documentation, you have to install `pipenv` first:
$ sudo apt install pipenv

After that, you can use the following targets (dependencies will be installed
automatically on the first use)

- Use 'make server' to run a local web server to preview the documentation at
  http://localhost:8000/
- Use 'make html' or 'make pdf' to build the documentation.
- Use 'make clean all' to build both the html and pdf version from scratch.

Deploying the website
---------------------

The website (https://simu5g.org) is served by GitHub Pages from the
Unipisa/Simu5G repository. It is deployed by pushing to the 'deployed-website'
branch of that repository:

$ git push <unipisa-remote> <commit>:deployed-website

The push triggers the "Build and deploy sphinx docs to GitHub Pages from site
branch" workflow (.github/workflows/sphinx-doc-build-deploy.yml), which runs
'make html' in doc/src and publishes doc/src/_build/html/. The workflow file
is taken from the pushed commit itself. Pages is only configured on the
Unipisa/Simu5G repository; pushing a 'deployed-website' branch to a fork does
not deploy anything.

The NED documentation (neddoc/) is NOT built by the workflow. It is downloaded
from the simu5g-X.Y.Z-doc.tgz asset of the vX.Y.Z GitHub release on
Unipisa/Simu5G, and copied next to the generated pages. The version is
determined as follows:

- on a push, it is the latest release of Unipisa/Simu5G, as reported by
  'gh release view' (drafts and pre-releases excluded), with the leading 'v'
  stripped;
- when the workflow is started manually from the Actions tab, the optional
  'doc_version' input overrides that; leave it empty for the latest release.

The release must actually carry the -doc.tgz asset, otherwise the download
fails and the deployment is aborted (rather than silently publishing the NED
documentation of some other version). The asset is created by 'releng/makedist'
from doc/neddoc/, and has to be uploaded to the release manually.

Note that the version of the NED documentation and the version displayed on the
pages have different sources: the latter comes from 'git describe' on the
deployed commit (see 'release' and 'version' in conf.py). They agree when the
deployed commit is a release, and differ otherwise.

The 'deployed-website' branch may carry commits that are not on the main
branches (e.g. site-only doc fixes). Merge the new content into it rather than
force-pushing over it, or bring those commits over first.

